breast cancer drugs fail miserably in  phase 2 clinical trials one of the  reasons being that the modeling that's  done in the beginning to test these  drugs is not very useful our research  has shown that the human immune system  is incredibly important in synergizing  with cancer therapy to kill Uman tumors  human breast tumors especially so what  our model does is it takes into account  the human immune system in testing new  cancer drugs and we believe that this is  the missing link  before I became affiliated with women's  health research at Yale I was doing very  basic cancer research and then this idea  popped into my mind in a conversation  with a few of my colleagues about  developing a humanized model of breast  cancer so in this model we were able to  produce the human immune system in mice  and then study breast cancer and its  treatment on top of that human immune  system our most important finding is  that radiation and the end the human  immune system work together to kill  cancer cells and this is really  important because the development of  this humanized model of breast cancer  will enable us to test novel drugs that  can be used to treat cancer better  our preliminary data showing that the  human immune system interacts with  radiation to kill tumor cells really got  one of our collaborators very interested  and she happens to be a breast surgeon  so she's going to supply us with breast  tumors from women who were in her clinic  right out of the operating room we can  take fragments of a woman's breast tumor  put them into a mouse that has her  immune system and then test novel drugs  and drugs that are currently operational  in the clinic on her breast tumor to see  which one's actually killed the tumor  and then she'll be able to be treated  with those drugs and I really feel like  this has a better chance of working than  anything else that's out there  without this critical funding this  critical pilot funding I wouldn't have  been able to carry out this research  develop the humanized model and many of  my colleagues would not be able to do  what they're doing in order to improve  the quality of life for women now we can  use those preliminary data funded by  women's health research at Yale to apply  for larger funding from the National  Institutes of Health and from larger  foundations in order to move the  research for it we think that this model  will enable us to identify cancer drugs  that will actually work in women and be  highly effective in curing breast cancer  you  you  you
 

